// MentalMap — Firestore security rules.
//
// Not wired to any CLI/deploy tooling (this repo has no build system and no
// firebase.json) — paste this into Firebase Console → Firestore Database →
// Rules → publish. This should be the last time: the rule below is
// deliberately field-shape-agnostic, so it never needs to change again no
// matter how the app's schema evolves.
//
// Person records are stored as plain fields — there is no client-side
// encryption (see firebase-sync.js for why). Owning your own uid's subtree
// is therefore the only real protection, and it's also the only thing this
// rule checks: previous versions also allow-listed the exact field names
// each collection could contain (hasOnly([...])), which meant every schema
// change in the app — a renamed field, a new settings checkbox, a new
// subcollection — needed a matching manual re-paste into the Firebase
// Console. That step kept getting missed, and every single time it was,
// every write silently failed with permission-denied — the actual root
// cause behind more than one "my map disappeared" investigation in this
// project's history. The field allow-list never added real security (only
// the account owner, or someone with their Firebase Auth credentials, can
// ever write here regardless of which fields are allowed) — it was
// defense-in-depth against malformed data, which the client already
// validates and guards against on its own (see the malformed-record checks
// in app.js). Dropping it trades that redundant, fragile check for a rule
// that can't drift out of sync with the code ever again.

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /users/{uid}/{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;
    }

    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
